Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether a countably infinite set of itineraries forces the entropy $h(T,P)$ to be zero.

**Answer: Yes, $h(T,P)=0$.**

## Proof

**Setup.** Let $\phi:X\to k^{\mathbb{N}}$ be the itinerary map, $\phi(x)=(i_0,i_1,\dots)$ where $T^n(x)\in P_{i_n}$. Then $I=\phi(X)$, which is countably infinite by hypothesis. Let $\mu=\phi_*\nu$ be the pushforward measure on $k^{\mathbb{N}}$, and let $\sigma$ denote the shift. Since $T$ is $\nu$-preserving and $\phi\circ T=\sigma\circ\phi$, the measure $\mu$ is $\sigma$-invariant. The entropy satisfies $h(T,P)=h_\mu(\sigma)$, since the refined partitions $P^{(n)}=\bigvee_{j=0}^{n-1}T^{-j}P$ correspond under $\phi$ to the cylinder partitions $\mathcal{Q}^{(n)}$ of $k^{\mathbb{N}}$, giving $H_\nu(P^{(n)})=H_\mu(\mathcal{Q}^{(n)})$ for all $n$.

**Step 1: $\mu$ is purely atomic.** Since $I=\phi(X)$ is a countable subset of the Polish space $k^{\mathbb{N}}$, it is Borel (a countable union of closed singletons). Moreover,
$$\mu(I)=\phi_*\nu(I)=\nu(\phi^{-1}(I))=\nu(X)=1.$$
So $\mu$ is supported on the countable set $I$, hence is purely atomic: $\mu=\sum_{y\in I} a_y\,\delta_y$ where $a_y=\mu(\{y\})\geq 0$ and $\sum_{y\in I}a_y=1$.

**Step 2: Every atom with positive mass is periodic.** Let $S_+=\{y\in I : a_y>0\}$. For any $y\in S_+$, the singleton $A=\{y\}$ has $\mu(A)=a_y>0$. By the Poincaré recurrence theorem (applied to the $\sigma$-invariant measure $\mu$), for $\mu$-a.e. $x\in A$, we have $\sigma^n(x)\in A$ for infinitely many $n\geq 1$. Since $A=\{y\}$, this means $\sigma^n(y)=y$ for infinitely many $n$. In particular, $y$ is periodic.

**Step 3: Decomposition into periodic orbit measures.** The periodic orbits $\{O_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}$ contained in $S_+$ are pairwise disjoint (distinct periodic orbits never intersect), and $\mathcal{A}$ is countable. Set $b_\alpha=\mu(O_\alpha)=\sum_{y\in O_\alpha}a_y$, so $\sum_\alpha b_\alpha=\mu(S_+)=1$.

We claim all points in the same orbit $O_\alpha$ have equal $\mu$-mass. Indeed, $\sigma$ maps each periodic orbit to itself, and distinct orbits have disjoint images (if $\sigma(y)=\sigma(z)=w$ with $y\in O_\alpha$, $z\in O_\beta$, then $w\in O_\alpha\cap O_\beta$, forcing $\alpha=\beta$). Hence for $z\in O_\alpha$, the unique $\sigma$-preimage of $z$ within $S_+$ is the predecessor of $z$ in $O_\alpha$. Invariance $\mu(\sigma^{-1}\{z\})=\mu(\{z\})$ then gives $a_{\sigma^{-1}z}=a_z$, so all weights in $O_\alpha$ are equal. Denoting by $\mu_\alpha$ the uniform (ergodic) measure on $O_\alpha$, we obtain
$$\mu=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}} b_\alpha\,\mu_\alpha.$$

**Step 4: Entropy vanishes.** Each $\mu_\alpha$ is supported on a single periodic orbit, so $h_{\mu_\alpha}(\sigma)=0$. By affinity of measure-theoretic entropy,
$$h_\mu(\sigma)=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}} b_\alpha\, h_{\mu_\alpha}(\sigma)=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}} b_\alpha\cdot 0 = 0.$$

Since $h(T,P)=h_\mu(\sigma)$, we conclude $h(T,P)=0$. $\blacksquare$

